eginning as you went through the  workshop container there were a lot of  all kind of machines to work with metal  screws you will put those things to use  in the future i'm sure but what about  forest and land management tools wood  chipper brush cutter chainsaws  yeah good question that is definitely  lacking those tools  but the tools we got in our workspace  basically came because we already had a  workspace back into the netherlands so  it's a bit of a mixture of it's a  workspace but also just moving our own  stuff  so yeah we decided to not buy any of  this in the netherlands but just see  here what we actually need and then just  slowly expand our workspace with more  landscaping tools  but yeah i would highly recommend if  you're going to start a project like  this and you don't have a metal late yet  don't get it get yourself a chainsaw or  something  when will you start making houses out of  plastic bricks  yeah  as soon as possible the kitchen building  looks really makeshift no straight walls  big gaps that are filled with concrete  missing bricks why renovating instead of  rebuilding i would say in general  because we prefer to reuse the things  you could even take the whole building  apart and recycle the bricks but  realistically it always demolishes it a  bit more  it breaks the parts so ideally we use  things in their original condition but i  have no clue if it would make sense with  the kitchen actually to do this we're  still looking into that right now can  you make a playlist that starts at  episode 1 so your journey can be watched  in order yes done that especially for  you how much price and how big is the  land there  we actually made a video about that on  our other channel i would highly  recommend to watch because it has all  the details  and in general we have this two channels  this is more for weekly updates and the  other one is more for the long-term  documentation  what does it mean prototyping a more  sustainable way of living not sure i  guess that's why we call it a prototype  you often say sustainable but what do  you actually mean by that ecologically  sustainable economically sustainable  physically sustainable  all of the above and in which case do  you decide to balance them  yeah very good question  complex as well i don't have the answer  to be honest but i do think that's what  we mean with prototyping so for instance  in today's world we often really focus  on the details of things so for instance  let's say you could design a stove that  is slightly more efficient so everyone  that cooks at home uses less energy  but sometimes it feels we might just  have to rethink how we cook because  instead of everyone individually cooking  maybe it makes more sense if you share  your meals so you don't need all this  individual stove you don't have all  these individual waste streams you don't  have all the individual  energy usage  but it's very hard to integrate in the  usual world and i think at project camp  we're starting a bit more from scratch  so we could really rethink how we live  and how we set up our daily routines  because it affects the way you eat the  way you use your infrastructure what you  do in your free time like i feel all  these topics are connected and how to  live sustainable but yeah very hard to  integrate in an existing system so here  it feels like we can prototype and  really tweak all these topics in a more  holistic approach  but yeah not sure how it's going to go  what makes project camp different than  all the other projects out there  yeah probably that we have highway  sounds i didn't hear that before  no but i do think this element of  community you have a lot of people going  as a couple living off grid kind of  looks like we did that until now i guess  but i think this really starts to get  excited when the community kicks in and  it becomes about  sharing resources and  infrastructure which we would start  doing more soon  when will project camp get the band back  together and or accept more volunteers  yeah so we're slowly approaching that as  a base camp is being set up we're still  not sure how to manage this because yeah  it also comes with some organization but  i would say soon what's the maximum  amount of people you're expecting to  have on the land at one time  i think we had no clue when we came here  like even  10 hectares like how many people could  live there what makes sense that you're  that is not chaos all the time but you  still have enough private space  as we're here we're starting to slowly  see it more  uh i still don't have an exact number i  think it could host quite some people  uh maybe like max 50 or something in  order it's for it to still be nice  but yeah we're still going to take a  while as well before we can host that  many people  how do you keep yourself entertained are  you lonely sometimes what is your social  life like  good question  so yeah personally i am pretty good in  entertaining myself on my own i always  see a thousand things i could do rita is  a bit more a social being so she really  likes to have people around so at this  moment it's a bit more hard for her  but i would say as he knows more people  are coming which makes it okay  but in general a lot to do so it's not  very easy to get bored but i do think we  need to figure out these things more  like what are our routines how do we  take off on this project and what do we  do in our free time how are you feeling  is living this way you expect it to be  uh yeah i guess i don't know what my  expectations were it also feels like  this is not really how we're gonna live  it's still very rough and raw now so it  feels very temporary the things we're  currently doing  but overall it's nice to spend more time  in nature the mental strain of what have  i got into during the rain season looked  like it sucked for a bit how are you  doing now apart from the highway saints  how is the spirits yeah i think they go  up and down  especially because we're sort of  analyzing this land and sometimes we  have these issues like the highway  sounds or the rain that you know they  affect us but also everyone that's gonna  go to project camp sort of be affected  by that so they feel quite heavy which  brings the spirits down  but then other days uh we have good  things  so i would say overall it's kind of  intense  but  yeah i guess that's part of it  do you want to make project come  something like a modern farm or rather  something like a rural lab or workshop  similar to fresh plastic or something  entirely different  do you have a concrete vision or are  planning things as you go yeah so we  would love to have more of these topics  on this land but realistically  integrating something like fresh plastic  here is quite challenging because you  first need to run your own basic life  having enough resources for yourself but  then also to run for instance  three-phase machines to do  experimentations and stuff and hosting  the people and making sure they have a  meal  it still feels like far away so in the  future it would be cool but it might  still take some time  is there anything that you learned  working on fresh plastic that is really  helping you with this project  yeah i think a lot actually during  pressure plastic version 4 where we  brought a lot of people together  to work on a shared mission  and that was really great i mean again  we made this video about it  so i would highly recommend to watch if  you're interested  but we learned a lot of uh community  lessons from that and i think it also  made it less scary to do this project  because you kind of feel like with the  right people you you get anything done  is the forks real so close yes very real  they actually come almost every evening  when we're in the town center they're  just walking by having a look playing  with some things that we left there it's  super nice what's your plan dealing with  all the invasive mimosas on yo